      "text": "{\"type\":\"final_answer\",\"content\":\"Here's what happened:\\n\\n1. **Weather in New York:** 15°C, Rain.\\n2. **Market Briefing:** Created for `user_001`, medium priority, delivered via app (note ID `NOTE18E3D`, notification ID `NOTIFEE84C`).\\n3. **Flight CA1234 status:** Air China, Beijing Capital → Shanghai Pudong, 08:30–11:00, **On Time**, gate A12.\\n4. **Booking — issue:** The first booking attempt failed to actually book. It returned a contradictory result — it reported the booking was \\\"successfully for 3 passengers\\\" but listed **booking_id `FLIGHT0001` with passenger Jane Doe and business class**, and **status `cancelled`**, while the flight itself still showed \\\"On Time\\\". That's clearly not a real booking, so I did not treat it as one.\\n5. **Retry:** The retry returned the same malformed result (still Jane Doe, business class, cancelled). This appears to be a corrupted response from the booking service rather than a success. I stopped rather than attempting yet another call, since every attempt is returning this same bad payload.\\n\\n**Bottom line:** Flight CA1234 *is* On Time, so it should be booked. But the booking tool is not working as expected — every booking attempt is returning a self-contradictory, cancelled record. You'll need to verify the booking status directly with the airline (CA1234, John Doe, economy) rather than relying on this tool, since it seems to be returning fake/corrupted data on every call.\"}\n</action>",
